"A new memory is weak. More than a hundred years ago, a German scientist named Hermann Ebbinghaus memorized lists of nonsense words and then tested himself again and again. He found that forgetting is fastest at the start. Much of a new memory can be gone within days, unless you do something about it.": "39810e1c5fda",
"Close the book and try to recall the list.": "34ae048689a3",
"Deep inside your brain, one on each side, lie two small curved parts called the hippocampus. Each is about five centimeters long and shaped a little like a seahorse, and that is what the name means. When something new happens, the sight is handled in one part of your brain, the sound in another, and the meaning in another. The hippocampus ties these pieces together into one memory.": "3fd1fcb0d3b6",
"Do not forget your umbrella.": "6c03ef8f22dc",
"How does sleep help you remember?": "acc24c3ab051",
"I practice the piano every day.": "1e26525e7e14",
"I remember my first day of school.": "11ecd0687541",
"Let's review the new words before lunch.": "af91bf5773c2",
"Please repeat the word after me.": "72d13cca914b",
"Riding a bike is a different kind of memory. You cannot explain it in words, and you do not forget it for years. Skills like this depend on other parts of the brain, including the cerebellum at the back, and they grow by doing, not by reading. So here is how to learn anything, including English words: use it, spread it out, sleep on it, and do it again. Try the word experiment below the model and see for yourself.": "564ed9097eb4",
"Sleep does the rest. While you sleep, your brain replays what you learned, and little by little the memory is moved out to the outer layer of the brain for long-term keeping. You learned in the lesson about sleep that a good night after studying helps you remember. Staying up late to study steals the very time your brain needs to save the work.": "10b982664bfc",
"That trip is my happiest memory.": "1697dbdde029",
"We followed the path through the trees.": "4a29b1075de3",
"What can you do? Use it. Every time you pull a memory out of your own head, the path gets stronger. That is why covering the page and testing yourself works better than reading the page again. Reading again feels easy, but your brain does little work. Recalling feels hard, and the hard work is what makes the memory stay. It also helps to spread your practice out: ten minutes on each of three days beats thirty minutes in one night.": "a90798e39de7",
"What did you have for breakfast today? What is your best friend's name? How do you ride a bike? You answered all three in a moment, but each answer was stored in a different way. Remembering feels like one simple thing. It is really several things, and you can get better at all of them.": "501e7677e09a",
